Beryllium: a test switch that turns off what a confined renderer does to the descriptors it receives

Purpose: plan/18 section 0.3 (adversarial review plan/16, finding BER-05;
  feature matrix S-IPC-01). A confined renderer limits every descriptor it
  receives, and the sender cuts every descriptor before sending it. Only
  the sender's cut is a boundary: a compromised renderer need not run its
  own code. While both are on, a test cannot tell which of the two did the
  work. Feature BerylliumReceiveSideLimits, on by default, is the receiver's
  half: with --disable-features=BerylliumReceiveSideLimits,
  LimitReceivedDescriptor() returns at once, as it would in a renderer that
  had been taken over. Every descriptor in every renderer must then still
  be inside the policy by procstat -C, or some sender is not cutting.
  - base/posix/capsicum_rights.{h,cc}: the feature, and the early return.
    The feature is read once, the first time a confining process receives a
    descriptor, which is after its feature list exists; the browser never
    reads it there. When it is off the process says so once in the log, so
    a run made that way cannot be taken for an ordinary one.
  What it does not touch: the sweep of the descriptor table at the lockdown
    point, the limits on socket pairs a confined process makes itself, and
    everything a sender does.
  This is a switch for tests. Nothing is gained by using it otherwise, and
    it joins the switches a production package ignores once the lockdown is
    the default (plan/18 section 3.3).
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on beryllium-descriptor-rights.patch.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled (written for build 0.20.3.21).
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py, class SenderAlone,
  test_the_senders_cut_alone_keeps_the_policy: the lockdown on and this
  feature off, every descriptor of every renderer inside the policy, and
  the renderer's own line in the log saying its limits are off.
security-review: required (descriptor rights in confined processes)

